  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Saturday)\nIssued at 1250 PM CDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nStronger southerly winds are expected Sunday and Monday as the\ngradient tightens over the region east of lee side low pressure.\nLower dewpoints on Sunday in combination with the near record\nwarmth and stronger winds will yield elevated fire weather danger\nacross northeast Oklahoma into far northwest Arkansas. A fire\ndanger statement will likely be needed. Winds will be slightly\nstronger Monday, but dewpoints will be on the rise as well. High\nspread indices still suggest elevated fire weather danger will\npersist into Monday as well. The latest guidance suggests that \nrecord warmth is looking more probable on Tuesday ahead of a front\ndropping south over KS. Fire spread potential will be lower due \nto lessening wind but should be at least of some concern.\n\nShower and storm chances increase Tuesday night into Wednesday in\nthe vicinity of the front and in advance of an upper trough\napproaching from the west. Models have been struggling with the\npattern evolution next week since it deals with the phasing of two\ndifferent streams of the westerlies. A portion of the\ndeterministic and ensemble data ejects the southern stream system\nnortheast into the Plains by late next week while phasing with a\nnorthern stream trough diving southeast out of Canada. Some data\nholds some southern stream energy back to the west, with the\nnorthern stream energy effectively cutting off the system. The\nmajority of the data shows a deep upper trough evolving over the\neastern CONUS by the start of next weekend, however. The model\nblend forecast has the highest PoPs in the Wednesday into Thursday\ntime frame as the front works its way through, decreasing toward \nthe end of the week. This appears reasonable at this point. There\nwill be potential for heavy rainfall with multiple rounds of rain\nand storms possible through that time in a plume of richer deep\nlayer moisture streaming north from Mexico (possibly some moisture\nfrom Simon?). Also, if some of the model solutions such as the 12Z\nGFS are closer to verifying, the ejecting southern stream\nshortwave could increase severe weather potential over the Plains\nalong/ahead of the front Wednesday into Thursday.\n\nWith the majority of the data developing a deep upper trough over\nthe eastern CONUS by next weekend, its looking more probable that\nmuch cooler and more seasonable temperatures will arrive along\nwith a return to quieter weather.\n\nLacy",
